Two are particularly interesting.

A physical polarizing filter can suppress reflections that ordinary software editing cannot fully recover after capture. Canon Academy +1

And background-oriented schlieren techniques can reveal airflow-related refractive changes by analyzing distortions in a patterned background. Springer +1

The key abstraction: Sometimes the right transformation is not an algorithm. It’s a different sensor.

A camera cannot retrieve temperature data it never recorded, just as an ordinary microphone cannot reconstruct frequencies beyond its bandwidth.

